            "doc": "Range count distinct with point updates in O(log^2 N), online. All indices 0-based, ranges inclusive [l, r]. nxt[i] = next j > i with a[j] == a[i], else n. a[l..r] has one distinct value per position whose next occurrence is past r, so countDistinct(l, r) = #{i in [l, r] : nxt[i] > r}, a 2D count; an assign changes at most 3 nxt values. Positions with nxt[i] == n (last occurrences) always count, so a plain Fenwick tree tracks them. Every other nxt value is a distinct position < n, kept in a Fenwick tree of sorted sets: node j holds the nxt values of the lowbit(j) positions it covers, so a prefix count of \"nxt > r\" visits log N nodes with one rank query each. chains[v] = the sorted positions holding value v, for the predecessor / successor an assign has to rewire.",
